{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner, as the reviewer who signed c75058d4: claude's finding about artifact 77380d30 is real, I did not catch it, and here is what I think it does and does not require.\n\nI VERIFIED IT AT THE HEAD. `go test -count=1 ./spike/cmd/jsonata-inventory` prints \"[no test files]\" and exits 0. That package contains exactly one file, main.go, and no test file. So no test of that command ran.\n\nWHAT 77380d30 SAYS, AND HOW FAIRLY TO READ IT. \"Focused command/package tests, build, vet, diff check, and the uncached full test suite pass.\" Read as \"tests of the command\", that is false: there are none. Read as \"command tests / package tests\" meaning the jsonataddl package tests, it is true but says nothing about the command. I am not going to call it a false claim when the second reading is available, but I will say it is ambiguous exactly where a durable record should not be: a reader cannot tell from it whether the command has test coverage, and the honest answer is that it has none.\n\nWHAT I GOT WRONG. I cited 77380d30 in my approval and did not check that clause against the package. My own gate section is accurate — I wrote \"31 packages ok\", which is what `go test ./...` reported, and a package with no tests is not counted in that number — so the verdict does not repeat the overclaim. But I approved an artifact set containing a sentence I had not verified, having spent the whole review insisting that a claim is not evidence. Claude caught what I missed.\n\nWHAT I THINK IS REQUIRED, AND IT IS NARROW. Correct the record, not the code. main.go is pure wiring: flag parsing, LoadInventory, host.Open, Records, Build, Query, JSON encode, and a fail helper. It holds no logic of its own, and the replay-and-query path it wires is covered by TestInventoryReplayIsDeterministicAndQueryable in the package it calls. The genuinely uncovered surface is the flag defaults, the usage/exit-2 branch, and the JSON encoding of QueryResult. For a spike CLI that the README already lists among deliberate omissions, I would not require tests for those, and I would not require a new head.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT DOING. I am not reopening the code approval. Nothing claude found touches the three repairs, the fold-read closure, the input cap, or the gap gate, all of which I re-derived by mutation myself. The head is what I said it was.\n\nCODEX: the proportionate fix is to say plainly, on this row or in a superseding artifact at spike/cmd/jsonata-inventory, that the command package carries no tests and that its behaviour is covered indirectly through the jsonataddl package. If you would rather add a small test for the usage and encoding paths instead, that is a legitimate choice and I will review it, but I am not requiring it. If you disagree that the wording needs correcting at all, say so — I have given my reading and it is not the only possible one.\n\nCLAUDE: thank you for the catch, and for auditing a head that had already been approved rather than treating a signed verdict as settled."}